# Line endings.
#
# These files were authored on Linux (LF) and are edited on Windows (CRLF).
# Without this file, every `git add` prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF",
# which is noise at best and, under a PowerShell script with
# $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop', enough to abort the run.
#
# Text is normalised to LF in the repository. Git still checks out whatever
# your core.autocrlf setting asks for, so nothing about your editor changes.

* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts stay LF like everything else. Windows PowerShell 5.1 and
# PowerShell 7 both run LF-only scripts without complaint, and forcing CRLF here
# only reproduces the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning this file exists to
# remove. Batch files are the exception: cmd.exe genuinely needs CRLF.
*.ps1  text eol=lf
*.psm1 text eol=lf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# Explicitly text, so a stray heuristic never treats them as binary.
*.py    text
*.toml  text
*.md    text
*.json  text
*.xml   text
*.cfg   text

# Never touch these - line-ending conversion corrupts them.
*.zip  binary
*.tif  binary
*.tiff binary
*.gpkg binary
*.kmz  binary
*.shp  binary
*.shx  binary
*.dbf  binary
*.png  binary
*.pdf  binary
